WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.062
Sorting helps us prioritize records and understand extremes.

00:00:04.062 --> 00:00:10.747
In this example, sort_values orders the DataFrame by amount from largest to smallest.

00:00:10.747 --> 00:00:16.107
Then nlargest returns the three rows with the largest amount values.

00:00:16.107 --> 00:00:19.192
Click Run and compare the two outputs.

00:00:19.192 --> 00:00:21.492
Sort_values keeps all rows but changes their order.

00:00:21.492 --> 00:00:27.147
Nlargest is useful when you only want the top few records.

00:00:27.147 --> 00:00:29.792
This pattern appears often in real work.

00:00:29.792 --> 00:00:34.842
You may want the highest-revenue products, the customers with the largest balances,

00:00:34.842 --> 00:00:39.527
the departments with the most incidents, or the top-performing campaigns.

00:00:39.527 --> 00:00:44.892
The opposite function, nsmallest, can help identify the lowest values.

00:00:44.892 --> 00:00:49.977
One caution: top values are not automatically the most important values.

00:00:49.977 --> 00:00:55.622
A large transaction may be valid, or it may be an outlier or data-entry error.

00:00:55.622 --> 00:00:58.512
Sorting helps you find what deserves attention.

00:00:58.512 --> 00:01:01.244
Interpretation still requires context.
